course: dictionary
lesson: 6596
title: One word, many places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The meaning that fits depends on the context, which means the words and sentences around it. Node is a good example: it can mean a meeting point, a lump, a part of a plant, or a computer in a network.
explain: «Lymph nodes, often called glands, swell while fighting an infection.» Swell and infection are the clues. Here a node is a small lump in your body, not a point on a map.
explain: «In Europa Universalis, Venice grows fat on its trade node.» Trade and grows rich are the clues. A node here is a busy point where trade meets, like a junction where routes join.
explain: Ask yourself: what does this node join, hold or connect? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A node is a point where lines, branches or paths meet or join.
fact: In science, a node can be a lump or swelling, such as a lymph node in the body.
fact: On a plant, a node is the place on a stem where a leaf or bud grows.
fact: In computing, a node is one device in a network, like a computer connected to others.
fact: In maths, a node is a point on a graph or network diagram where lines meet.
exercise: «Lymph nodes, often called glands, swell while fighting an infection.» What is a lymph node? | A computer in a network | *A small lump or swelling in the body | A point where paths meet on a map | A type of leaf
exercise: «Cuttings are made just below a node on the stem.» Where is a node on a plant? | The seed inside the fruit | *The place on a stem where a leaf or bud grows | A wet patch in the soil | The very top of the root
exercise: In a maths diagram, what does a node look like? | *A point where lines meet | A number on the axis | A long straight line | A circle with no lines
test: «Venice grows fat on its trade node.» What is a trade node most like? | A small bird that sings at dawn | *A busy point where trade routes meet | A river that never freezes | A wall around a city
test: «An ant's narrow waist has one or two small bumps called nodes.» What are the nodes? | Its eyes | Tiny wings for flying | Its six legs | *Small bumps on an ant's waist
exam: Jayden says node always means a computer. Is he right? | Only in games | Only in science | *No, node has several meanings, and the context shows which one | Yes, always
